[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now to the heart of your original ask: what "[1mtasks they care about[0m" means. This is the data model of the whole
  notification system, so it's worth getting right.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether caring is inferred or declared. [3mInferred[0m means the system decides for you — you're notified about
  tasks you created, were assigned, or commented on. Zero UI, matches what people expect, but it can't express "I'm not
  involved in this one but I need to know when it ships," and it can't express "stop telling me about this thread."
  [3mDeclared[0m means an explicit watch/unwatch button. It handles both of those cases, but people forget to press it, so the
  feed starts empty and stays empty, and the feature quietly dies.

  The reason nearly every tool that does this well (GitHub, Linear, Jira) ends up at [1mboth[0m is that each alone fails in
  practice: auto-subscribe when you become involved, plus a manual watch/unwatch that can override either direction.

  Cost of "both" over inferred-only: one subscription table instead of a derived query, and a watch toggle in the task
  UI. That's small. Going the other way later — retrofitting explicit watches onto an inferred-only model — means
  backfilling subscriptions for every existing task, which is the more annoying direction.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m both. Auto-subscribe on involvement, with manual override.
